"Represents regional totals with a fixed number of units per dot to show approximate geographic concentration.\n\nUse when: Communicate where quantities are concentrated without filling entire regions uniformly. Compare spatial patterns across several population categories.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need exact point locations or precise local counts from individual dots.\n\nVisual grammar: Every dot represents a declared fixed quantity. Dots are distributed within their valid reporting areas. Category colors, if used, keep dot value and placement rules consistent.\n\nBuild: Choose the reporting geography, categories and quantity represented per dot. Use justified placement constraints where uninhabitable or irrelevant areas should be excluded. Set dot size and density for the display scale and explain the approximation.\n\nCheck: Totals reconcile with the source within the stated rounding rule. Dots do not imply observed individual locations. Dot value, placement method and excluded areas are documented.\n\nPitfalls: Describing random symbol positions as actual household locations. Changing dot values between compared panels without a clear warning.\n\nExample: A regional population map uses one dot per stated number of residents, constraining placement away from water and comparing settlement concentrations.\n\nAlternatives: Choropleth map, Proportional-symbol map."
